## 7. Technology Stack (Finalized)

**Confirmed approach: Plain HTML, CSS, and JavaScript** (no framework, no CMS).

| Layer | Technology | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Structure | HTML5 | 4 static pages: `index.html`, `gallery.html`, `courses.html`, `contact.html` |
| Styling | CSS3 (single shared `style.css`, or per-page if needed) | Mobile-first, responsive via Flexbox/Grid + media queries |
| Interactivity | Vanilla JavaScript (`script.js`) | Nav toggle (mobile menu), gallery lightbox, form validation, WhatsApp message builder, **EN/Bengali language toggle** |
| Application Form Handling | Client-side JS validation + **WhatsApp click-to-chat link** (`wa.me`) — no backend, no database, no paid API | JS builds the message string from form fields and opens WhatsApp with it pre-filled; student taps Send to deliver |
| Hosting | Static hosting (e.g., shared hosting via cPanel, Netlify, GitHub Pages, or Vercel) | Since site is fully static HTML/CSS/JS, hosting cost and complexity stay minimal |
| Content Updates | Manual — developer edits HTML directly for course/gallery changes (no CMS) | Confirmed given no framework/CMS; revisit if client later wants self-editing |

**File Structure (proposed):**
```
/khan-institute-site
 ├── index.html
 ├── gallery.html
 ├── courses.html
 ├── contact.html
 ├── /css
 │    └── style.css
 ├── /js
 │    └── script.js
 └── /assets
      └── /images
```

